## Overview

**enhanced-video-sveltekit** is a pair of Vite plugins (markup transform + virtual module loader) that turn a single custom element in your Svelte files into **encoded video variants**, an **auto-extracted poster** (`jpg` plus optional `webp` / `avif`), and a small **runtime component** that handles lazy loading, optional click-to-play, autoplay discipline, and poster fade tied to real playback.

You write a static tag with a **string literal** `src`; the build probes the file with ffprobe, runs ffmpeg for each selected format and resolution, deduplicates work via a **content-hashed disk cache**, and emits optimized assets. The browser receives `<picture>` for the poster and ordered `<source>` elements so it picks the first format it can play.

<Callout title="npm name" tone="info">

The package on npm is **`enhanced-video-sveltekit`**. Internal names (`?enhanced-video`, `/@enhanced-video/`, cache folder `enhanced-video`) stay short on purpose.

</Callout>

## Requirements

| Requirement | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| **Node** | ≥ 18 |
| **Svelte** | ^5 |
| **Vite** | ^6.3 or ≥ 7 |
| **@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte** | ^6 or ^7 |
| **ffmpeg / ffprobe** | On `PATH` at build time, or pass `advanced.ffmpegPath` / `ffprobePath`, or install `ffmpeg-ffprobe-static` |

<Callout title="ffmpeg" tone="warning">

Encoding runs **during dev** (background + HMR) and **synchronously on production build**. Missing ffmpeg or a failed encode **fails the build**. Install a build that includes **libx264**, **libx265** (HEVC), **libvpx-vp9**, and **libsvtav1** (AV1) if you enable those formats.

</Callout>

## Install

<DocsInstallSwitcher />

```bash
# optional: vendored binaries so CI machines do not need system ffmpeg
pnpm add -D ffmpeg-ffprobe-static
```

## Vite setup

Add **`enhancedVideos()`** from `enhanced-video-sveltekit` **before** the SvelteKit (or `vite-plugin-svelte`) plugin so the markup transform runs ahead of compilation. If you use Tailwind’s Vite plugin, keep the order shown in this demo:

```ts
import tailwindcss from '@tailwindcss/vite';
import { sveltekit } from '@sveltejs/kit/vite';
import { enhancedVideos } from 'enhanced-video-sveltekit';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
	plugins: [tailwindcss(), enhancedVideos({ resolutions: [360] }), sveltekit()]
});
```

This demo passes **`resolutions: [360]`** so cold encodes stay quick; the default ladder is **`[1080, 720, 480]`** (variants taller than the source are skipped).

## Authoring in Svelte

Use the custom element in any **`.svelte`** file (not in raw `.svx` / markdown — put the tag in a `.svelte` wrapper if you author MDsveX). Standard **`<video>`** attributes pass through (`autoplay`, `muted`, `loop`, `controls`, `playsinline`, `class`, `style`, `preload`, ARIA, events, bindings). A user-supplied **`poster`** attribute is ignored; the pipeline always generates one.

<Callout title="Static `src` only" tone="warning">

`src` **must be a string literal**. Dynamic `src={variable}` is rejected with a clear error — the transform has to resolve the file at build time.

</Callout>

Minimal example (angle brackets escaped here so this docs file can live next to the same transform):

```svelte
&lt;enhanced:video src="./hero.mp4" autoplay muted loop playsinline />
```

### Runtime props (beyond `<video>`)

These are handled by the generated **`EnhancedVideo`** wrapper (see `enhanced-video-sveltekit/runtime`):

| Prop | Default | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `loading` | `lazy` | **`lazy`**: gate `<source>` elements behind **IntersectionObserver** until near the viewport. **`eager`**: load immediately. **`click`**: poster + play control until the user starts playback. |
| `preload` | `metadata` | Forwarded to the underlying `<video>`. |
| `autoPause` | `true` | Pause when the block scrolls out of view; resume when it returns. |
| `respectReducedMotion` | `true` | Suppress autoplay when `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`. |
| `playLabel` | `Play video` | Accessible label for the click-to-play control when `loading="click"`. |

## What gets encoded

Default **`formats`** are **`['mp4', 'webm']`**: **H.264 + AAC** in MP4 and **VP9 + Opus** in WebM. You can add **`av1`** and **`mp4_hevc`**. The runtime emits `<source type="…">` in **declaration order** — put the format you want modern browsers to try first (for example AV1 before H.264).

| `formats` value | Container | Typical use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `mp4` | MP4 (H.264 + AAC) | Broad compatibility |
| `webm` | WebM (VP9 + Opus) | Smaller than MP4 for many clips |
| `mp4_hevc` | MP4 (H.265 + AAC) | Smaller MP4; Safari-oriented tagging |
| `av1` | WebM (AV1 + Opus) | Best compression where decode exists |

Posters are extracted to **jpg** (always) plus **webp** / **avif** when encoders allow, behind **`<picture>`** for efficient delivery.

## `enhancedVideos(options?)`

| Option | Type | Default | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `formats` | `VideoFormat[]` | `['mp4', 'webm']` | Output codecs to emit |
| `resolutions` | `number[]` | `[1080, 720, 480]` | Target heights in px |
| `quality` | `'web' \| 'balanced' \| 'archive'` | `'balanced'` | Preset bundle (CRF, encoder speed, poster quality, audio bitrate) |
| `cacheDirectory` | `string` | `node_modules/.cache/enhanced-video` | Disk cache root |
| `maxJobs` | `number` | `max(1, cpus - 1)` | Concurrent ffmpeg jobs |
| `advanced` | `object` | `{}` | Hardware acceleration, fps cap, binary paths, per-codec overrides, lock TTL |

### `advanced` highlights

| Field | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `hwAccel` | `'auto'` \| `'videotoolbox'` \| `'nvenc'` \| `'vaapi'` \| `'qsv'` \| `false` — applies to **H.264 / HEVC**; VP9 and AV1 stay software |
| `fps` | Cap output frame rate |
| `ffmpegPath` / `ffprobePath` | Override discovery |
| `overrides` | Per-codec / poster tweaks on top of `quality` (e.g. `h264.crf`, `av1.preset`, `poster.jpg`) |
| `lockMaxAgeMs` | Stale lock cleanup (default 2h) |

### Quick recipes

```ts
// faster iteration / CI
enhancedVideos({ quality: 'web' });

// smaller files, longer encodes
enhancedVideos({ quality: 'archive' });

// AV1 first, then VP9 WebM, then H.264 MP4
enhancedVideos({ formats: ['av1', 'webm', 'mp4'] });

// single rung on the resolution ladder
enhancedVideos({ resolutions: [720] });

// reproducible software-only encodes
enhancedVideos({ advanced: { hwAccel: false } });
```

## Programmatic metadata

Import the same pipeline without the custom element:

```ts
import meta from './clip.mp4?enhanced-video';
// EnhancedVideoMetadata: width, height, duration, poster { jpg, webp?, avif? }, sources[]
```

Use this when you want to build your own `<video>` markup or pass data elsewhere.

## Dev vs production

- **Dev:** first load can kick off background encodes; the app may briefly use placeholders while middleware serves assets with **HTTP range** support. Finished jobs **invalidate** modules so HMR picks up new URLs.
- **Build:** all variants for all discovered sources are encoded **before** the build finishes; failures fail the build.

## Cache

Artifacts live under **`node_modules/.cache/enhanced-video/`** (or your `cacheDirectory`), in **one subdirectory per content hash**. The hash folds in **source bytes**, **encoder args**, **resolved quality profile**, **package version**, and **ffmpeg version banner** — change any of those and you get a new folder. There is **no automatic pruning**; delete the cache directory to reclaim space.

